<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Ms. Barton is a 52-year-old nurse in continuation treatment for recurrent major depression, in remission on sertraline 100 mg, who asked for a same-day visit. Two days ago, while helping unload crates of supplies at the agency warehouse, she felt a sudden pull across the lower back. The pain is an ache across both sides of the low back, 6 of 10, worse when bending or getting out of a chair, and eased by lying still or walking slowly. It does not travel down either leg. She has no numbness, weakness, trouble passing urine, loss of bowel control, fever, weight loss, or history of cancer, and she has not fallen. She has taken acetaminophen, which helps a little. Her mood has stayed good; she has kept her daily walks shorter and is worried about losing her routine. She has had no suicidal thoughts. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Two days ago, lifting supply crates</td></tr><tr><td>Location</td><td>Low back on both sides; no radiation into the legs</td></tr><tr><td>Severity</td><td>6 of 10; worse with bending and rising from a chair</td></tr><tr><td>Red flags</td><td>No numbness, weakness, urinary or bowel change, fever, weight loss, cancer history, or fall</td></tr><tr><td>Depression</td><td>In remission on sertraline 100 mg; mood good; no suicidal thoughts</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

