  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute low back strain (S39.012A)</content>. Acute mechanical low back pain after lifting, with no red flags and a normal neurological examination. Imaging is not indicated. Most cases improve within a few weeks. [S]<list><item>Stay active and keep walking within comfort; avoid bed rest; heat for 20 minutes several times a day.</item><item>Ibuprofen 400 mg up to three times a day with food for no more than 7 days. Sertraline with an NSAID raises the risk of stomach bleeding, so take it with food, stop it if she has black stools, vomiting of blood, or abdominal pain, and take no other NSAID or aspirin.</item><item>No lifting over 10 kg for two weeks; ask colleagues to unload the crates.</item><item>Return if pain spreads down a leg, or at once for weakness, numbness in the groin, or a change in bladder or bowel control.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Major depressive disorder, recurrent, in remission (F33.1)</content>. Remains in remission on sertraline 100 mg. Pain and a break in routine can unsettle mood in early remission. Safety reviewed: no suicidal thoughts, intent, or plan; protective factors unchanged. [S]<list><item>Continue sertraline 100 mg.</item><item>Keep the shorter daily walk and her monthly peer support group.</item><item>Collaborative safety plan reviewed and unchanged; 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline and the clinic number confirmed.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Major Depressive Disorder (2022); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); Noninvasive Treatments for Acute, Subacute, and Chronic Low Back Pain: A Clinical Practice Guideline From the American College of Physicians (2017)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Return if not improving in four weeks or at once for red-flag symptoms. Preventive visit in October. Next scheduled visit 2026-10-13: periodic adult preventive visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
